    "ls|LS17_S6_U_162": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 162  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nBOARD FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT \n \n162. DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is planning to set targ ets for percentage of people \nskilled as it is there in the case of literacy; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the rea sons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to set up a  board for skill development \nthat is similar to the Central Board of Secondary E ducation to ensure the \nassessment and standard of skill development and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether there are any incentive mechanisms to ensure employers take part in \nactive recruitment and also in the process of skilling and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana  (PMKVY) is the flagship skill \ntraining scheme of the Ministry of Skill Developmen t and Entrepreneurship (MSDE). \nUnder PMKVY 2.0 (2016-2020), a target to train one crore individuals was set. The \nnumber of candidates trained is 1.09 crore. Under P MKVY 3.0 (2020-2022), which \nwas launched on 15.01.2021, a target to train 8 lak h individuals has been set. The \nnumber of candidates trained so far is 1.2 lakh. \n \n(c): MSDE, vide notification dated 5 \nth  December 2018, has set up National Council \nfor Vocational Education and Training (NCVET) as an  overarching regulatory body \nfor the skill eco-system. It is mandated to regulate the functioning of entities engaged \nin vocational education and training, both long and  short-term, and establish \nminimum standards for the functioning of such entities. \n \n(d): Under PMKVY scheme, various initiatives are ta ken to ensure better placement \noutcomes for the trained/ certified candidates. Rojgar Melas  are conducted for \nplacement of youth by industry. MSDE has launched A atmanirbhar Skilled \nEmployees Employer Mapping (ASEEM) portal, which ac ts as a directory of skilled \nworkforce. The objective is to provide a platform t hat matches supply of skilled \nworkforce with the market demand, thereby facilitat ing better livelihood opportunities \nfor youth and availability of ready skilled manpower to employers. \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_163": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 163 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nVacant posts in IITs and NITs \n \n†163. SHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of institutes where the post of chairman and other posts are \n lying vacant in the governing board of the IITs and NITs across the \n country;  \n \n(b) whether any time limit has been fixed for filling up these vacant posts;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a):   At present, 8 posts of Chairman at IITs and 21 posts of Chairman at \nNITs are vacant. Further, 5 posts of Directors in IITs and 5 posts in NITs are \nvacant.   \n(b) to (d ): The nomination/selection of Chairman and other posts is a \ncontinuous process.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_164": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 164 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nJagdishpur-Haldia Gas Pipeline Project  \n \n164.    MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the status of Jagdishpur-Haldia Gas pipeline project; \n(b) the tentative timeline for supply of CNG for the city of Kolkata; and \n(c) the plans and timeline for supplying piped cooking gas in Kolkata? \n  \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयमऔर\u0000ाकृितकगैसमं\u0000ालयम\u0000रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी \n (\u0000ीरामे\u0000वरतेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) has authorized 2,655 Km long \nJagdishpur-Haldia-Bokaro-Dhamra Natural Gas Pipeline (JHBDPL) to GAIL (India) Limited to \nconnect eastern parts of the country. About 1,986 Km of pipeline has been laid as on 31.03.2021.  \n \n(b) & (c) Bengal Gas Company Limited (BGCL) has been authorized for the development of \nCity Gas Distribution (CGD) network in the Geographical Area (GA) of Kolkata Municipal \nCorporation and parts adjoining districts of North 24 Parganas, South 24 Parganas, Howrah, \nHooghly and Nadia. Supply of Natural Gas to the GA has started. Development of the CNG \nstations /PNG connections in a GA is done as per the terms and conditions of the authorization.  \n \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_165": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.165\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH JULY, 2021\nFRAMEWORK FOR ORGAN TRANSPLANTATION\n*165. DR. ARVIND KUMAR SHARMA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that around four lakh people die in India every\nyear waiting for an organ transplant and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government has taken note of various difficulties being faced by the\nreceiver viz., finances for transplant, post transplant medication, legal complexities,\nnon uniform definitions, lack of necessary infrastructure, information gap and by t he\ndonor like lack of awareness;\n(c) if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government has formulated/proposes to formulate any robust\nframework to facilitate organ transplant; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and the steps/measures taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 165* FOR 30TH JULY, 2021\n(a) to (e) The Government of India has taken note of difficulties posed by shortage of\navailability of organs for transplantation in the country and has taken various measures\nto address the same.\nThe Government has enacted Transplantation of Human Organs (Amend ment)\nAct 2011 to amend the Transplantation of Human Organs Act (THOA), 1994 and\nnotified Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Rules 2014. The aforesaid Act\nand Rules have various provisions for increasing the pool of organ donors and to do\naway with legal complexities as well as non-uniform definitions.\nGovernment is implementing the National Organ Transplant Programme\n(NOTP) for augmenting organ donation and transplantation in the country. A three\nlevel framework of networking organisations with NOTTO at National level, Regional\nOrgan and Tissue Transplant Organisation (ROTTO) at regional level and State Organ\nand Tissue Transplant Organisation (SOTTO) at State level, has been set up for\nproviding an efficient and organized system of organ and tiss ue procurement from\ndeceased donors and their allocation to the waiting recipients.\nSo far, NOTTO at New Delhi, 5 ROTTOs (at Chandigarh, Mumbai, Chennai,\nKolkata and Guwahati) and 14 SOTTOs (in the State of Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Goa,\nGujarat, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Jharkhand, Kerala, Madhya\nPradesh, Odisha, Punjab, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh) have been established.  As on date\n595 centres (503 transplant centres and 50 retrieval centre and 42 tissue banks) are\nlinked with the National Registry maintained by NOTTO.\nNOTP has various provisions which include:\n Financial assistance for setting up of State Organ and Tissue Transplant\nOrganisations (SOTTOs) in each State/UT.\n Financial assistance for setting up of Bio-material centres.\n Financial assistance for establishing new Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities\nand strengthening of existing Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities.\n Training to Manpower including surgeons, physicians, anesthetists, transplant\ncoordinators, etc.\n Financial assistanc e for hiring of Transplant Coordinators to medical colleges\nand trauma centres.\n Financial assistance for maintenance of bodies of deceased donor.\n\n Financial assistance to Below Poverty Line (BPL) transplant recipients, who\nhave undergone transplant in a Government Hospital, for post -transplant\nmedications.\nA National Bio -material Centre has been set up at NOTTO, New Delhi and a\nRegional Bio -material centre has been established at ROTTO, Tamil Nadu. Further,\nfunds have been released to the State of Bihar a nd Maharashtra for establishment of\nBio-material Centre. Funds have also been released to the State of Bihar, Punjab for\nestablishing new Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities and strengthening of existing\nOrgan Transplant/retrieval facilities.\nFurther, under Rastriya Arogya Nidhi, financial assistance is being provided to\npoor patients who are suffering from life threatening diseases relating to heart, kidney,\nliver, etc. for their treatment including respective organ transplantation at Government\nhospitals/institutes having super specialties facilities.\nAs far as creating awareness activities are concerned the NOTTO, ROTTOs and\nSOTTOs carry out awareness and disseminate relevant information about organ\ndonation to the public. A website www.notto.gov.in and a 24x7 call centre with a toll\nfree helpline number (1800114770) have been made operational. A number of\nactivities, for generating awareness are carried out from time to time. These include\ncelebration of Indian Organ Donation Day ev ery year, seminars, workshops, debates,\ndisplay boards on organ donation awareness in the hospitals, sports events,\nwalkathons, participation in marathons, nukkad natak, etc. Social media is also being\nused to create awareness about organ donation among the citizens.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_166": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 166 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.07.2021 \n  \nGLOBAL GENDER GAP REPORT \n  \n*166. SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA: \nSHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \n  \n     Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:       \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has taken note of ranking of India in Global Gender Gap \nreport and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the steps taken by the Government to bridge the gender gap in India; and \n(c)  the steps taken to improve health as well as economic and political participation of \nwomen in India? \n  \n  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n (a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 166 FOR 30.07.21 BY SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH \nVERMA AND SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI REGARDING ‘GLOBAL \nGENDER GAP REPORT’ \n                                   \n(a). Yes, Sir. As per the Global Gender Gap Report 2021 published by the World \nEconomic Forum, India is ranked at 140 out of 156 countries with a score of 0.625 ( out of \n1). The Global Gender Gap Report  provides the scores on Global Gender Gap Index \n(GGGI) which examines the gap between men and women in four dimensions, namely, \nEconomic Participation and Opportunity, Educational Attainment, Health and Survival and \nPolitical Empowerment.  According to the Global Gender Gap Report 2021, India has \nscored 0.326 in Economic Participation and Opportunity, 0.962 in Educational Attainment, \n0.937 in Health and Survival and 0.276 in Political Empowerment. \n  \n(b) and (c) Government of India has given utmost priority to bridge the gender gap in India, \nreducing disparity between men and women, improving the health of women, increasing \ntheir socio-economic status and participation in various fields. Some major initiatives taken \nby Government of India for removing the gender gap in all aspect of social, economic and \npolitical life are as follows: \n \n \n  \nEconomic Participation \n  \no Beti Bachao Beti Padhao ensures the protection, survival and education of \nthe girl child. \no Scheme for Adolescent Girls aims to empower girls in the age group 11 -18 \nand to improve their social status through nutrition, life skills, home skills and \nvocational training \no Working Women Hostel ensures the safety and security for working women. \no The National Crèche Scheme ens ures that women take up gainful \nemployment through providing a safe, secure and stimulating environment to \nthe children. \no radhan Mantri Awaas Yojana aims to provide housing under the name of \nthe       woman also. \no Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana aims to enable a large number of \nIndian youths including women to take up industry -relevant skill training in \nsecuring a better livelihood. \no Deen Dayal Upadhyay National Urban Livelihoods Mission focuses on \ncreating opportunities for women in skill development, lea ding to market -\nbased employment. \no Sukanya Samriddhi Yojna - Under this scheme girls have been economically \nempowered by opening their bank accounts. \no Skill Upgradation & Mahila Coir Yojna  is an exclusive training programme of \nMSME aimed at skill development of women artisans engaged in coir \nIndustry. \no ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������-linked \nsubsidy programme aimed at generating self -employment opportunities \nthrough establishment of micro-enterprises in the non-farm sector. \no Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana provides access to institutional finance to \nmicro/small business. \n  \n\n \n \nHealth and Nutrition \n  \no Government has announced  Mission Poshan 2.0  to strengthen nutritional content, \ndelivery, outreach and outcomes with focus on developing pr actices that nurture \nhealth, wellness and immunity to disease and malnutrition. \no Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandna Yojna aims to provide maternity benefit to pregnant \nand lactating mothers. \no Implementation of Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child, Adolescent Heal th plus \nNutrition (RMNCAH+N) \no Rollout of Comprehensive Primary Health Care including health promotion through \nAyushman Bharat-Health & Wellness Centers (AB-HWC) \no Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram (JSSK) to eliminate out -of-pocket expenses \nfor pregnant women delivering in public health institutions and sick infants \naccessing public health institutions for treatment. \no Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) to provide financial assistance to pregnant women \nfor encouraging institutional delivery. \no Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan (SUMAN) to provide assured, dignified, respectful \nand quality healthcare at no cost and zero tolerance for denial of services \no Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) to provide comprehensive \nand quality ANC to pregnant women on the 9th of every month. \no LaQshya initiative to improve the quality of care in Labour room and Maternity \nOperation Theatres. \no Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana empowers women and protects their health by \nproviding LPG cylinder free of cost. \n \nPolitical Participation \no To br ing women in the mainstream of political leadership at the grass root level, \ngovernment has reserved 33% of the seats in Panchayati Raj Institutions for \nwomen. Capacity Building of Panchayat Stakeholders including Elected Women \nRepresentatives is conducted  with a view to empowering women to participate \neffectively in the governance processes. \n  \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n  \n ",
