    "ls|LS17_S6_U_169": " \n������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n��\n��������� �\n���������������������� ���\n������������������������������� �� ������� �������\n� �����������������������\n������������ �\n��\n�������������������������������� ���\n������������������� ��� ����������� ��\n������������������������������� ���\n������������������� � ������� ���\n� �������� ���������������� ���\n��������������������� ���\n��������������������������� ���\n���������������������� ���\n��������������������������� ��\n��\n��������������������������������������������������\n��\n���� � ����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������\n���� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ��������������������� �� ����� ��� ��������� �����������������������\n��������������������������������������������������\n��\n������ �\n���������������������� ���������������������� ��\n���������������������� ��\n��\n���� ��������������� ��� ��������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������� ��\n���������������������������\n������\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_17": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.17 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nNew Kendriya Vidyalayas in Maharashtra  \n \n*17.  SHRI VINAYAK RAUT:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is planning to set up new Kendriya \nVidyalayas (KVs) in Sindhudurg and Ratnagiri Districts of \nMaharashtra;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(d)  the criteria laid down for opening of such new KVs? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n****** \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nTHE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 17 RAISED BY SHRI \nVINAYAK RAUT , HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO BE \nANSWERED ON 19 TH JULY 2021 REGARDING NEW KENDRIYA \nVIDYALAYAS IN MAHARASHTRA.  \n \n(a) to (d) Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has not received any \nproposal in the prescribed proformae for opening of a new KV in \nSindhudurg and Ratnagiri Districts  from the State Government of \nMaharashtra. \n \nOpening of Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) is a continuous process. \nKVs are opened primarily to cater to the educational needs of the wards \nof transferable Central Government Employees including Defence & \nPara-Military personnel, Central Autonomous Bodies, Central Public \nSector Undertakings (PSUs) and Central Institute s of Higher Learning \n(IHL) by providing a common programme of education. Proposals for \nopening of new KVs are considered only if sponsored by Ministries or \nDepartments of the Government of India / S tate Governments / Union \nTerritories (UTs) Administrations and committing resources for setting up \na new KV. The proposals received from various sponsoring authorities \nfor opening of new KVs have to compete with other proposals under the \n“Challenge Method”. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_170": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.170\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH JULY, 2021\nWAITING PERIOD IN AIIMS\n†*170. SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT:\nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a large number of patients have to wait for at least a year for getting\ntreatment in AIIMS, particularly for ailments like cancer, neurology and kidney\ndisease;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether it is true in respect of OPD consul tation also in addition to the surgical\nprocedures; and\n(d) the steps being taken by the Government to minimise the waiting period for\ntreatment of various critical diseases in such institutions?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 170* FOR 30TH JULY, 2021\n(a) to (d) AIIMS, New Delhi, which is the oldest functional AIIMS, faces a demand -\nsupply mismatch in terms of far more number of patients needing hospitalisation vis-à-\nvis the number of beds available. To minimise the waiting period of treatment of\nvarious critical diseases, various projects such as Mother and Child Block, National\nCentre for Ageing, surgical block and paid Private Wards have been added.  In the\ncasualty/ emergency life -saving situations, all efforts are made to admit the seriously\nill patients in the hospital as far as practically possible. Various clinical departments\nmake their waiting list of patients needing admission according to the patient’s\ncondition, the urgency of treatment required and bed availability.\nAs far as OPD is concerned, registrations are done as per maximum handling\ncapacity of the concerned Speciality / Department. 80% of OPD appointments for new\npatients are being provided online for upto 30 days in advance and remaining 20% of\nOPD appointments are being provided for wa lk-in patients. In addition, patients given\ndates by their doctors are provided appointments through Single Window Exit Counter\nand departmental nodal persons. As far as follow up patients are concerned, patients\n(with prior appointments as well as walk -in) are registered / seen by respective OPD’s\nwithout any limit.\nIn order to reduce the load of patients on AIIMS, New Delhi and to correct\nregional imbalances in availability of tertiary healthcare services, setting up of 22 new\nAIIMS in different parts of the country has been approved under PMSSY.  Six of the\nAIIMS at Bhopal, Bhubaneswar, Jodhpur, Patna, Raipur and Rishikesh are already\nfully functional. The current patient load is being treated satisfactorily and in a timely\nmanner in these new AIIMS.\n************",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_171": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.171 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF KUMARAKOM AS ICONIC TOURIST \nDESTINATION \n \n171. SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether th e Government has declared certain Tourism \ndestinations including Kumarakom in Kerala as Iconic Tourist \nDestinations and if so, the details thereof with copy of the \nnotification of tourism department;  \n(b)  Whether the Government has given directions to pre pare \ndetailed project report for Kumarakom Iconic Tourist \nDestination and if so, the details thereof with the name of the \nagency who is assigned with the work;  \n(c)  whether the Government has received the draft report of the \nKumarakom Iconic Tourism Proje ct and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(d)  whether the Government has received any proposal to inclu de \nKottayam-Kumarakom-Cherthala Tourist Highway and the \ndevelopment of the canals in the project and if so, the details \nthereof along with the expected total cost of the project; and  \n(e)  whether the Government has set apart any fund for the iconic \ntourism destinations and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF TOURISM            (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Tourism has framed “ Development of \nIconic Tourist Destinations Scheme”, for development of nineteen \nidentified iconic destinations including Kumarakom (Kerala) in the \ncountry following a holistic  approach. The scheme is under \nsubmission  for  approval of  competent  authority . No process has  \nbeen  initiated  to prepare  a detailed  project  report  for  \n\nKumarakom as  Iconic  Tourist  Destination.  However  a  draft  \nmaster plan  has  been  prepared  by the  Ministry of  Tourism for  \ndevelopment of  Kumarakom through the  cons ortium  of  M/s INI \nDesign Studio - Jones Lang Laselle Property Consultants India Pvt. \nLtd and is under consultation with stakeholders.  \n \nAs the scheme is not approved, no details regarding \ndevelopment projects and the funds sanctioned in this regard have \nbeen finalized . However, in the  Expenditure Finance  Committee  \n(EFC) meeting held on 15.10.2020  an  amount of  Rs. 5109  Crore for \nthe  period  of  Financi al Year 2020 -21  to  2025 -26  has  been  \nrecommended  for  development of  19 Iconic  Tourist  Destinations.     \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_172": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.172\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH JULY, 2021\nSETTING UP OF AIIMS HOSPITALS\n*172. SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN:\nSHRI V. K. SREEKANDAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the guidelines issued/norms put in place to set up AIIMS Hospitals in the country;\n(b) the present status of completion/commencement of AIIMS Hospitals sanctioned by\nthe Government, State/UT-wise;\n(c) the funds sanctioned and released for development of all the AIIMS Hospitals in\nthe country during the last five years and the current year, State/UTwise;\n(d) whether there is any proposal to set up AIIMS hospital in Kinalur in Kozhikode in\nview of limited availability of tertiary healthcare centres in North Kerala and also in\nNanded in Maharashtra; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 172* FOR 30TH JULY, 2021\n(a) to (e) Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yogna (PMSSY) aims to establish All\nIndia Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) in all the States in the Country in a phased\nmanner. Setting up of 22 AIIMS has been sanctioned so far under the Scheme. Six of\nthe sanctioned AIIMS at Bhopal, Bhubaneswar, Jodhpur, Patna, Raipur and Rishikesh\nare fully functional. Status in respect of remaining 16, including funds released during\nthe last five years and the current year is at Annexure.\nThe envisioned facilities at each new AIIMS inter alia include a 960 or 750\nbedded hospital (610 beds in case of AIIMS, Raebareli), facilities for teaching in\nUnder Graduate (UG) and Post Graduate (PG) Medical Education and Nursing, 18\nSpecialty and 17 Super Specialty Departments.\nIn Maharashtra, One AIIMS at Nagpur has already been sanctioned. Apart from\n22 new AIIMS, which have been sanctioned under PMSSY since its inception,\nproposals for setting up AIIMS at other places have also been received. AIIMS are\nbeing set up in various parts of the country keeping in view gaps in tertiary healthcare\ninfrastructure in different parts of the country.\n\nAnnexure\nStatus of new AIIMS under PMSSY\nS.N\no.\nState/UT AIIMS Date of\nCabinet\nApproval\nApprov\ned Cost\n(Rs. in\ncrore)\nPresent Status Fund\nreleased\nduring the\nlast 5 years\n(Rs. in\ncrore)\nFund\nreleased\nduring the\ncurrent\nyear (Rs.\nin crore)\n1 Andhra\nPradesh\nAIIMS,\nMangalagiri\n07.10.2015 1618  Progress of\nconstruction work:\n✓ Phase I - OPD\nBlock & Residential\nComplex: 99.5%\n✓ Phase II – Hospital\nand Academic\nCampus: 76%\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2018 -\n19.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n12.3.2019.\n869.74 42.28\n2 Assam AIIMS,\nGuwahati\n24.05.2017 1123  Construction work in\nprogress- 48.85%\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2020 -\n21 from a temporary\ncampus.\n435.08 0.35\n3 Bihar AIIMS,\nDarbhanga\n15.9.2020 1264  Encumbrance free\nland yet to be handed\nover by State Govt.\n- -\n4 Gujarat AIIMS,\nRajkot\n10.01.2019 1195  Construction work in\nprogress- 6%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2020 -\n21 from a temporary\ncampus.\n166.56 10.16\n5 Haryana AIIMS,\nManethi\n28.02.2019 1299  Encumbrance free\nland yet to be handed\nover by State Govt.\n- -\n6 Himachal\nPradesh\nAIIMS,\nBilaspur\n03.01.2018 1471.04  Construction work in\nprogress- 66%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2020 -\n21.\n858.14 89.03\n\n7 Jammu &\nKashmir\nAIIMS\nJammu\n10.01.2019 1661  Construction work in\nprogress- 22%\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2020 -\n21 from a temporary\ncampus.\n370.68 -\n8 AIIMS\nKashmir\n10.01.2019 1828  Construction work in\nprogress- 4.5%\n253.67 -\n9 Jharkhan\nd\nAIIMS,\nDeoghar\n16.05.2018 1103  Construction work in\nprogress- 48%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20 from a temporary\ncampus.\n379.94 110.68\n10 Maharas\nhtra\nAIIMS,\nNagpur\n07.10.2015 1577  Progress of\nconstruction work:\n✓ Phase I - OPD\nBlock & Residential\nComplex: 100%\n✓ Phase II – Hospital\nand Academic\nCampus: 93%\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2018 -\n19.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n9.9.2019.\n935.63 67.38\n11 Punjab AIIMS,\nBathinda\n27.07.2016 925  Construction work in\nprogress- 86.10%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n25.12.2019.\n668.4 0.36\n12 Tamil\nNadu\nAIIMS,\nMadurai\n17.12.2018 1264  Pre-investment work\nin progress.\n Loan agreement\nsigned between Govt.\nof India & Govt. of\nJapan.\n12.35 -\n13 Telangan\na\nAIIMS,\nBibinagar\n17.12.2018 1028  Pre-investment work\nin progress.\n Executing Agency for\nthe main work\nappointed.\n Master Plan finalised.\n Tender floated.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20.\n OPD services\n23.85 -\n\noperationalised since\n2.6.2020.\n14 Uttar\nPradesh\nAIIMS,\nRaebareli\n05.02.2009\n[Revised\nCost\nEstimates\n(RCE) was\napproved\nby EFC on\n22.06.2017]\n823  OPD & Residential\nblock completed.\n Medical College /\nHospital under\nconstruction\n✓ Progress – 100%\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n13.8.2018.\n665 -\n15 AIIMS,\nGorakhpur\n20.07.2016 1011  Construction work in\nprogress- 86.05%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n24.2.2019.\n755.12 0.45\n16 West\nBengal\nAIIMS,\nKalyani\n07.10.2015 1754  Progress of\nconstruction work:\n✓ Phase I - OPD\nBlock & Residential\nComplex: 96%\n✓ Phase II – Hospital\nand Academic\nCampus: 90.5%.\n MBBS classes\ncommenced from\nacademic year 2019 -\n20.\n OPD services\noperationalised since\n27.1.2021.