�� �����������������\n����� �����������������������������������\n����� ����������������������������������������� �\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������ ��� ������ ��� ������� ��� ��� ������� ����� ��� ���� ������������ ��� ����������\n�������� ��� ���� ������������ ���������������� ������������� ��� �������� ��� ��������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ����������������������������������������������������\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������� ������ �������� �������� ��� ����� ��� ��������� ��� ����� ����� ������� ����\n�������������������������\n����� ������������������������������� �����������������������������������������������\n������������\n����� �����������������������������������������������\n����� ��� ����������������������������������������������������������\n����� ��� �������������������������������������������������������\n����� ����� ������������\n����� ���������������\n����� �������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������������������������������������������������",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_180": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 180 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nMINIMUM WAGE FOR TEA GARDEN WORKERS \n \n180.  SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the detailed reasons and criteria for considering geographical \nlocation for fixing of the floor wage under Code on Wages, 2019; \n(b)the State - wise details of reasons on the basis of which the floor \nwage shall be fixed and tentative duration within whic h the \nGovernment shall release the state- wise fixed floor wage; and \n(c)the method to determine minimum wage for tea garden workers in \nAssam as compared to other tea producing States? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) & (b): Section 9(1) of the Code on Wages, 2019, provides that the \nCentral Government shall fix floor wage taking into account minimum \nliving standards of a worker in such manner as may be prescribed; \nprovided that different floor wage may be fixed for diffe rent \ngeographical areas. It is stated that the provisions of the Code on \nWages, 2019, have not come into force.  \n \n(c):  Under the Minimum Wages Act, 1948, the fixation of \nminimum wages for the tea garden labourers, falls under the purview \nof State Government which is the appropriate Government to fix/revise \nthe minimum wages. The tea plantation workers are paid wages as per \nthe negotiated agreement reached through a process of collective \nbargaining between the Producer Associations and Workers Union s \nunder the aegis of the respective State Governments. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_181": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *181  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.08.2021  \n  \nTRAINING PROVIDERS ENROLLED WITH NSDC  \n  \n*181.     DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL:  \n SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE:  \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state:  \n  \n(a)       the details of training providers enrolle d with National Skill Development \nCorporation (NSDC) under the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal  Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) since \n2015 and the procedure for enrolment of such training providers, State-wise;  \n  \n(b)       the beneficiaries trained by these empane lled training centres along with \npersons trained and employed under the scheme since 2015, State-wise;  \n  \n(c)        whether a target was fixed for its two c omponents, if so, the details of target set, \nyear-wise, since 2015 and the basis on which the es timation has been made that \ncorresponding target was met; and  \n  \n(d)       the details of monitoring mechanism to en sure that a person is enrolled, trained \nand employed under PMKVY?  \n  \nANSWER  \n  \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n  \n(a) to (d)         A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n  \n\n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 181 TO BE ANSWERED ON 02.08.20 21 ASKED BY DR. \nSUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL AND SHRI DHAIRYASHEE L \nSAMBHAJIRAO MANE  REGARDING TRAINING PROVIDERS ENROLLED WITH \nNSDC  \n \n(a)       Under Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana , short term training is being \nimparted through empanelled training centres. The e mpanelment of training centres \n(TCs) is being done online through Skill India Port al (SIP) combined with a third party \ninspection which is same for all the TCs irrespecti ve of the States. The State-wise \nnumber of empanelled TCs under PMKVY (i.e. PMKVY 1. 0, 2.0 and 3.0) since 2015 is \ngiven in Annexure I . \n \n(b)           PMKVY has two components namely Short  Term Training (STT) and \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL). Under STT comp onent, candidates are being \ntrained in short duration courses through empanelle d TCs whereas under RPL \ncomponent, candidates with prior learning experienc e or skills are being oriented and \ncertified. Under PMKVY since inception i.e. 2015, 6 5.95 lakh candidates have been \ntrained under STT and 63.98 lakh candidates have been oriented under RPL.  \n \nSTT is linked to placement, while RPL does not mand ate placements as it \nrecognizes the existing skills of candidate. Under STT-PMKVY, as on 10.07.2021, 52.46 \nlakh candidates have been certified; out of which, 23.25 lakh candidates have been \nplaced.  \n \nThe State-wise number of candidates trained, certif ied and placed under STT \nthrough empanelled TCs and candidates oriented under RPL under PMKVY since 2015, \nas on 10.07.2021, is given in Annexure II.  \n \n(c)        Yes Sir. Under PMKVY, targets for STT an d RPL have been fixed phase wise. \nThe phase wise and component wise target is given below:  \n \n[figures in Lakhs]  \nPhase  STT  RPL  Total  \nPMKVY 1.0 (2015 -16)  14  10  24  \nPMKVY   2.0 (2016 -20)  60  40  100  \nPMKVY 3.0 (2021 -22)  2.2  5.8  8 \nTotal  76.2  55.8  132  \n \n            Out of total target to train 132 lakh c andidates, 129 lakh candidates have been \ntrained/oriented.  \n \n(d)       Under PMKVY 2.0, there are various provis ions for monitoring of Training \nCentres (TC) as well as candidates. The life cycle of training process of candidates \n(enrollment-training-assessment-certification-placement) is tracked or monitored on \nreal-time basis through the Skill India Portal (SIP ) which is linked with Aadhaar enabled \nbiometric attendance. Empanelled TCs are being moni tored effectively through various \nmethodologies such as self-audit reporting, call va lidations, surprise visits, etc. \nAppraisal of the on-going schemes and consequent im provements is an ongoing \nprocess.  \n \n            Further, in order to improve the monito ring under PMKVY, District Skill \nCommittee (DSC) is being involved and entrusted wit h the monitoring under PMKVY \n3.0. DSC acts as an on-ground monitoring agency for  conducting inspection of \nempanelled TCs. \n   \n\nAnnexure I  \n  \nState-wise number of empanelled TCs under STT-PMKVY 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0, as on \n10.07.2021,  \n  \nState/UT  PMKVY 1.0  PMKVY 2.0  PMKVY 3.0  Grand \nTotal  \nAndaman And Nicobar Islands  3 12  4 19 \nAndhra Pradesh  783 530  75 1,388 \nArunachal Pradesh  16 105  22 143 \nAssam  312 393  92 797 \nBihar  519 502  91 1,112 \nChandigarh  31 55  6 92 \nChhattisgarh  188 172  37 397 \nDelhi  405 384  41 830 \nGoa  6 25  11 42 \nGujarat  249 423  63 735 \nHaryana  546 1,080  34 1,660 \nHimachal Pradesh  198 316  18 532 \nJammu And Kashmir  125 380  43 548 \nJharkhand  194 224  23 441 \nKarnataka  631 277  71 979 \nKerala  204 327  66 597 \nLadakh  0 8  0 8\nLakshadweep  0 1  1 2\nMadhya Pradesh  1,018 1,117  86 2,221 \nMaharashtra  681 881  109 1,671 \nManipur  14 131  42 187 \nMeghalaya  23 82  19 124 \nMizoram  11 79  17 107 \nNagaland  18 75  12 105 \nOdisha  451 387  58 896 \nPuducherry  44 50  8 102 \nPunjab  479 812  63 1,354 \nRajasthan  846 1,415  69 2,330 \nSikkim  11 35  7 53 \nTamil Nadu  1,145 1,071  109 2,325 \nTelangana  594 480  69 1,143 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman \n& Diu \n 7 18  4 29 \nTripura  108 175  9 292 \nUttar Pradesh  1,605 2,298  122 4,025 \nUttarakhand  97 371  28 496 \nWest Bengal  651 460  89 1,200 \nTotal  12,213 15,151  1,618 28,982 \n  \n  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure II  \n  \nState-wise number of candidates trained, certified and placed under STT and \ncandidates oriented under RPL under PMKVY since 2015, as on 10.07.2021,  \n  \nState  STT  RPL  \nTrained  Certified  Placed  Oriented  Certified  \nAndaman And Nico