ner  \n44 Rajasthan University of Veterinary & Animal Sciences, Bikaner \n45 SKN Agriculture University, Jobner \n46 Agriculture University, Kota \n47 Agriculture University, Jodhpur \nTamil Nadu \n48 Tamil Nadu Agricultural University, Coimbatore \n49 Tamil Nadu Veterinary & Animal Sciences University, Chennai \n50 Tamil Nadu Dr J Jayalalithaa Fisheries University , Nagapattinam \nTelangana \n51 Sri Konda Laxman Telangana State Horticultural University, Hyderabad \n52 Sri PV Narsimha Rao Telangana Veterinary University, Hyderabad \n53 Professor Jayashankar Telangana State Agricultural University, Hyderabad \nUttarakhand \n54 G.B. Pant University of Agriculture & Technology, Pantnagar \n55 VCSG Uttarakhand University of Horticulture & Forestry, Bharsar \nUttar Pradesh \n56 Chandra Shekhar Azad University of Agricultural & Technology, Kanpur \n57 Narendra Dev University of Agriculture & Technology, Faizabad  \n58 Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel University of Agriculture & Technology, Meerut \n59 U.P. Pt. Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Pashu Chikitsa VigyanVishwaVidhyalaya \nEvem Go Anusandhan Sansthan, Mathura \n60 Banda University of Agricultural and Technology, Banda \n\n5 \n \nS.No. Name of University \nWest Bengal \n61 Bidhan Chandra Krishi Viswa Vidhyalaya, Mohanpur \n62 West Bengal University of Animal & Fishery Sciences, Kolkata \n63 Uttar Banga Krishi Viswavidhyalaya, Cooch Behar \n \nCentral Agricultural Universities  \n64 Central Agricultural University, Imphal \n65 Rani Lakshmi Bai Central Agricultural University, Jhansi \n66 Dr. R.P. Central Agricultural University, Pusa, Samstipur, Bihar \n \nDeemed Universities  \n67 Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi \n68 Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Mumbai \n69 Indian Veterinary Research Institute, Bareilly \n70 National Dairy Research Institute, Karnal \n \nCentral Universities with Agricultural Faculty  \n71 Aligarh Muslim University, Aligarh \n72 Nagaland University, Medziphema \n73 Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi \n74 Vishva Bharti, Sriniketan \n \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_237": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 237 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.08.2021 \n \nSILAPATHAR – BAME (ALONG) LINE \n \n*237. SHRI PRADAN BARUAH: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has initiated a new Broad Gauge Line \nfrom Silapathar-Bame (Along); and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including Gauge, length and States \ncovered? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n \n(a) and (b):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 237 BY SHRI  PRADAN BARUAH  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 04.08.2021 REGARDING SILAPATHAR \n– BAME (ALONG) LINE \n \n \n(a) and (b):     Ministry of Defence has identified Broad Gauge line of \nSilapathar - Bame - Along falling in North Eastern Region for detailed \nstudy. Accordingly, Final Location Survey (FLS) and preparation of \nDetailed Proj ect Report (DPR) have been taken up.  As per field \nsurvey, total length of new Broad Gauge line Silapathar – Bame – \nAlong is 96.25 km. \n \nThe alignment passes through Assam and Arunachal Pradesh. The \nproject is not sanctioned. \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_238": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 238 \nANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2021 \n \nPMFME SCHEME \n \n238. SHRI BHOLA SINGH: \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:  \nSHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY:  \nSHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA):  \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \nSHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK: \nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \nDR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether the Government has started scheme of Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro  \nFood Processing Enterprises (PMFME) to enhance the competitiveness of existing  \nindividual micro-enterprises of the food processing industry; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government  has recently disbursed Rs.25.25 crore in the first year of the  \nPMFME scheme for meeting seed capital requirements of Self Help  Group (SHG), if so, \nthe d etails thereof and the funds sanctioned to the States, State -wise especially in  \nAndhra Pradesh; \n \n(d) whether the Government is promoting One District One Product (ODOP) and  \ndevelopment of Common Incubation Centres (CICs) under the PMFME in the country; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the total number of CICs allotted to the States especially  in \nAndhra Pradesh; \n \n(f) whether the Government has developed an online portal for submission of CICs  \nproposals and an online CICs map; and \n \n(g) the other steps being taken by Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n (SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) Yes Sir \n \n(b) As part of Aatmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyan, Ministry of Food Processing Industries has  \nlaunched a centrally  sponsored \"PM Formalisation of Micro food processing Enterprises  \n(PMFME) Scheme\" for providing financial, technical and business support for upgradation of  \nexisting micro food processing enterprises. The scheme was approved by the Union Cabinet  on \n20 May, 2020 and Guide lines for the scheme were issued on 19 June, 2020. It is to be  \n\n \nimplemented for a period of five years from 2020 -21 to 2024 -25 with an outlay of Rs.10,000  \ncrore. Two lakh micro food processing units are to be directly assisted with credit linked  \nsubsidy. \n \nThe Scheme adopts One District One Product (ODOP) approach to reap benefit of  \nscale in terms of procurement of inputs,  availing common services and marketing of products. \nOne District One Product approach would provide framework  for value chain development and \nalignment of support infrastructure. \n \nSupport to food processing units, SHGs/ FPOs/ Cooperatives envisaged in the scheme is as \nunder: \n \n(i) Credit linked grant @ 35% of the project cost with maximum grant up to Rs.10 \nlakh to micro food processing units for capital expenditure; \n \n(ii) Credit linked grant @ 35% of the project cost to SHGs/FPOs/Cooperatives for \ncapital expenditure; \n \n(iii) Seed capital @ Rs.40,000/- per member of SHGs engaged in food processing \nas a working capital; \n \n(iv) Credit linked grant @ 35% of the project cost for common infrastructure; \n \n(v) Support for marketing & branding up to 50% of the expenditure. \n \n(c) In the FY 2020 -21, amount of Rs. 376.67 Cr. had been released to States forPMFME  \nScheme, which included the amount for Seed Capital. For FY 2021-22, amount of Rs. 91.03 Cr. \nhas been released to States. For the State of Andhra Pradesh in FY 2020 -21, the amount of  Rs. \n34.98 Cr. has been released including amount for Seed Capital. In FY 2 021-22, the amount of \nRs. 14.15 Cr. has been released for Seed Capital. State-wise details are at Annexure-I \n \n \nTill date 49209 SHG members have been recommended amounting Rs. 139.18 Cr. to State  \nNodal Agencies(SNAs) by State Rural Livelihood Missions (SRLM ) under National Rural  \nLivelihood Mission (NRLM). In the State of Andhra Pradesh 7614 SHG Members have  \nrecommended amounting Rs. 29.78 Cr to SNA by SRLM. State -wise details are at Annexure- \nII. \n \n(d) to (g) Yes Sir.    Under the One District One Product (ODOP) component of the \nPMFME Scheme, the Ministry of Food Processing Industries has approved ODOP for 707  \ndistricts for 35 States and UTs, including 137 unique products as per the recommendations  \nreceived by States/UTs. The GIS ODOP digital map of India has been  launched to provide  \ndetails of ODOP products of all the States and UTs. \n \n54 Incubation Centers have been approved by MOFPI, details are at Annexure-III. One \nIncubation Center is approved in Andhra Pradesh in Chittoor District at IIT, Tirupati for  \nprocessing of its ODOP Tomato along with Mango and Citrus. Online portal has been  \ndeveloped by the Indian Institute of Food Processing Technology for the Ministry of Food  \nProcessing Industries for submission of Incubation Center proposals. The proposals ca n be  \nsubmitted    at: http://iifpt.edu.in/pmfme-cif-application/ and IC    map    may    be    found  on: \nhttp://iifpt.edu.in/olapp/pmfme/web/incubation.php. For institutes willing to apply for  Grant -\nin-Aid for establishment of IC under PMFME Scheme, assistance and guidance are  available at \nhttp://iifpt.edu.in/olapp/pmfme/web/incubation.php. \n \n \n***** \n \n\n \nAnnexure - I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 238 FOR ANSWER ON 20TH JULY,2021  \nREGARDING PMFME SCHEME. \n \n \nState-wise details of funds sanctioned under PMFME in the Financial Year 2020-21 & \n2021-22 \n \n \n \nSl \nNo. \n \nState \nFunds Sanction for FY 2020-21 \n(In Cr.) \nFunds Sanction \nfor FY 2021-22 \n(In Cr.) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands 1.8167  \n0.093 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 34.9839 14.1515 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0.1534 6.122 \n4 Assam 16.7052 3.8775 \n5 Bihar 9.0452 0.5085 \n6 Chandigarh 0.4 0.51 \n7 Chhattisgarh 4.6853 5.8