    "ls|LS17_S6_U_239": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) �������������������� �������������������������������������������� �������������������������������������������� ���� ������������������������ ���������������������������������������� ������������������������ \n�������������������������������������������� *239.DR. RAJDEEP ROY: Will the Minister of �������������������������������������� �������������� ������ �������� �������� ���� be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government plans to start a campus of the Indian Institute of Foreign Trade (IIFT) at Silchar considering the fact that Northeast shares 98% of its borders with foreign countries; and (b) if so, the details thereof? \n������������ ���������������������������������������������� ( �������������������������������� )���������������������������������������������� ( �������������������������������� )THE MINISTER OF  COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n(a) &(b):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. *****\n\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ���������������� �������� ������������ ������������������������������������������ ���������� ���������������������������� �������� ������������ ������������ �������������������� ���������������������������������������������� ����\n����������������������   There is no proposal of the Government of India to start a campus of Indian Institute of Foreign Trade (IIFT) at Silchar as of now. The needs of the North -East Region are being catered to by the Kolkata Campus of IIFT. ****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_24": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n24\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n \n20\nTH\n \nJULY, 2021\n \n \nNON\n-\nRECEIPT OF PM\n-\nKISAN FUNDS\n \n \n*24. \n  \nSHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is aware that a large number of farmers from Satara district \nare not receiving the benefit of PM Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM\n-\nKISAN) Yojana due to faulty \nor pending data entry or delay in verification; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the measures being take\nn to rectify the situation; and\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nthe grievance redressal mechanism available to the adversely affected farmers?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (c\n):\n \nA \nstatement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nc\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n24\n   \nDUE FOR REPLY ON\n \n20\nth\n \nJULY, 2021\n.\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nPM\n-\nKISAN Scheme is a continuous and ongoing Scheme. Under the PM\n-\nKISAN \nScheme benefits are transferred to eligible beneficiaries directly into their bank accounts as \nand when their correct and verified data is received from respective States/UTs Government\n \non PM\n-\nKISAN web portal and its validation through Aadhaar/PFMS/Income Tax database. \nThus, the data of eligible beneficiaries undergo various levels of verification and validation. \nOnce the process of verification and validation is complete, the benefits o\nf the Scheme are \nprovided to the intended beneficiaries.  \n \n \n \n \nSo far as, in the Satara district of Maharashtra, out of 5,47,512 registered farmers \n5,29,800 beneficiaries have received  benefit of  PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme. \n \n \n(b)\n:\n \n \nVarious facilities through Farmers\n \nCorner of PM\n-\nKISAN portal and CSCs have been \nprovided for the benefit of farmers through which they can get corrected their Aadhaar details \nand can also know about the status of benefit transferred. The State/UT Governments are \ncontinuously requested and \nvigorously pursued to organize publicity/awareness camps to \nensure expeditious Aadhaar authentication of the data of the beneficiaries along with \ncorrection of already registered data. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nMany remedies are available to the farmers who have not been \nable to register \nthemselves for benefit under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme or who have been registered but not \nreceived the benefits so far. The State Governments have appointed nodal officers for the \nscheme at block, district and State level to whom the farmers can su\nbmit their grievances. \nNormally, District Agriculture Officers or the District Collectors are available to attend to their \ngrievances.\n \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \nThe PM\n-\nKISAN portal is also utilized by farmers to lodge their grievances for their \nprompt redressal. \nAn \ne\nxclusive grievance mechanism “Help Desk” has been incorporated \nunder the “farmers’ Corner” of the PM\n-\nKIsan Portal, through which the grievance of the \nfarmer is directly transferred to the Nodal\n \nOfficer \nconcerned. Farmers can also register their \ngrievances \nthrough Public Grievance portal along with sending their grievances directly to the \nDepartment of Agriculture, Cooperation and Farmers Welfare. The Department ensures that \nthe grievances of farmers are resolved in a time bound manner. \n \n \nTo further help farmers in seeking redressal of their various grievances and replies to \ntheir various queries relating to the scheme a 24 x7 IVRS based help\n-\nline 155261/011\n-\n23381092 has also been set up. \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_240": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 240 \nANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2021 \n  \nPRIVATE SECTOR IN FPI \n  \n240. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to increase private sector participation in the Food \nProcessing Industries (FPI) to bring food processing revolution which is overdue for several \ndecades; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is aware that there is a need of post-harvest food processing revolution \nwith specific focus on value addition on each and every product produced in the country and for \nthis purpose Public Private Partnerships (PPP) mode is required; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and any existing plan in this regard; and \n \n(e)  whether the Government is considering to encourage setting up of food processing units near the \nfields and if so, the details thereof? \n  \n ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a) to (e):  Government has been consistently encouraging and  incentivizing increased private sector \ninvestment for overall development of Food Processing sector of the country to promote value addition \nin agricultural and allied sector and reduce wastage. \n \nMinistry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) is implementing a Central Sector umbrella \nscheme- Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana (PMKSY) since 2016 -17 for overall growth and \ndevelopment of food processing sector, mostly through private sector participation. Under the component \nschemes, MoFPI provides credit linked financial assistance (capital subsidy) in the form of grants-in-aid \nto entrepreneurs for setting up of food processing / preservation industries with farm level infrastructure \nlike primary processing facilities, collection centers etc.   So far, 792 projects out of a total of  818 \nprojects  have been approved for setting up food processing Industries by the private sector with approved \nGrants in Aid of Rs 5791.71 Crores. \n \n In order to further increase  private sector participation in the sector, Government has taken three \nmajor initiatives this year. Firstly, Government has approved the introduction of “Production Linked \n\nIncentive scheme” for Food Processing Sector with an outlay of Rs 10,900 Crore to support c reation of \nglobal food manufacturing champions and support Indian brands of food products in international market. \n \n Secondly, with a view to extend incentives to the private micro food enterprises, Self Help Groups \n(SHGs), Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs) and Cooperatives, spread all over the country, MoFPI is \nimplementing centrally sponsored \"PM Formalisation of Micro food processing Enterprises Scheme\". \nThe scheme aims to provide financial, technical and business support for upgradation/ setting up o f two \nlakh micro food processing units based on One District One Product (ODOP) approach in a period of \nfive years from 2020-21 to 2024-25 with an outlay of Rs. 10,000 crores. \n \nThirdly, in the budget speech for 2021 -2022, Government has announced expansion  of scope of \n“Operation Greens  scheme” from Tomato, Onion & Potato (TOP) to 22 perishable products, so as to \nboost value addition in agriculture and allied products and their exports. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_241": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*241\nANSWERED ON 05.08.2021\nCONSTRUCTION OF DAUDHAN DAM\n†*241.        SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the status of progress made in the action plan of displacement for Daudhan dam under Ken-Betwa Link\nProject;\n(b) the time by which the construction of the said dam is likely to be completed;\n(c) whether the Government has made any rehabilitation plan for the people residing in the ten villages of\nChhattarpur district which are likely to be submerged due to the construction of the said dam and canals\nunder the said project; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTs (a) TO (d) OF STARRED\nQUESTION NO. †*241 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.08.2021 REGARDING\n“CONSTRUCTION OF DAUDHAN DAM” RAISED BY SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA,\nM.P.\n(a) The proposed Daudhan dam under Ken -Betwa Link Project (KBLP) would submerge about\n9000 ha, affecting ten villages. The indicative plan for rehabilitation and resettlement of the project\naffected families is included in the Detailed Project Report (DPR).  The action plan for rehabilitation\nand resettlement of the affected families will be implemented after approval of the Cabinet for this\nproject.\n(b) As per the DPR, the entire project is expected to take eight years for its completion, including\nDaudhan dam.\n(c) & (d) A detailed rehabilitation plan has been included in the DPR for the project affected\npersons residing in the ten villages of Chhattarpur dis trict of Madhya Pradesh as per t he Right to\nFair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation a nd Resettlement Act,\n2013’. The total outlay proposed for the implementation of Project Affected Families' Econ omic\nRehabilitation Plan is Rs. 2,280 crore (at 2020 -21 price level) for Daudhan dam, Ken -Betwa Link\nCanal, Ken Left Bank Canal etc. This is included in the estimated cost of the project.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_242": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.242 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.08.2021 \n \nELECTRICITY TO POOR HOUSEHOLDS UNDER SAUBHAGYA \n \n*242.  SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE: \nSHRI GAUTHAM SIGAMANI PON: \n \nWill the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has launched various  schemes to provide \nelectricity to all poor households in  the country including Karnataka under \nPradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar Yojana - Saubhagya; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the ach ievements made along with the challenges  faced by the \nGovernment under Saubhagya and also the  details of other schemes launched \nby the Government to  provide electrification in urban areas of the country  \nincluding district-wise in Karnataka; \n \n(d)  the numbe r of households in rural and urban  areas which have been \nprovided with power facility in  the country during each of the last three years \nand the  current year, category -wise including Scheduled Castes,  Scheduled \nTribes and others; \n \n(e)  whether the Governme nt has achieved the targets  set for providing \nelectrification in rural and far flung  tribal and backward areas of the country \nincluding Karnataka, Uttar Pradesh and North-Eastern Region; and \n \n(f)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI  R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (f) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*********** \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( f) OF STARRED QUESTION    \nNO. 242 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON  05.08.2021 REGARDING \nELECTRICITY TO POOR HOUSEHOLDS UNDER SAUBHAGYA \n********* \n(a) to (c) : Government of India had launched Pradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar \nYojana-Saubhagya in October, 2017 with the objective to achieve universal household \nelectrification by providing electricity connections to all  un -electrified households in \nrural areas and all poor households in urban areas in the country.  \n \nUnder the Saubhagya scheme, as on 31.03.2021 all the States reported \n100% electrification of all the willing u n-electrified households, identified before \n31.03.2019.  A t otal 2.817 crore households have been electrified since the launch of \nSaubhagya, up to 31.03.2021. State -wise electrification of households including \nKarnataka since the launch of Saubhagya Yojana are at Annexure-I. \n \nThe details of challenges faced under Saubhagya scheme are as under:  \n \ni)    Households scattered in inaccessible & remote areas.  \n \nii)  Difficult & Hilly terrain, inclement weather, Riverine / Marshy / Snowbound . \n \niii)  Transportation of material by Head loading, Helicopters, Bamboo Bridges, Rafts, \nBoats, etc.  \n \niv)   In Left Wing Extremism affected areas  some hurdles were faced from the \nextremists.  \n \nv)   Forest clearance and Right of Way issues.  \n \n \nMinistry of Power (MoP), Government  of India launched the  “Integrated \nPower Development Scheme\" (IPDS) on 3 rd December’14 for strengthening the power \nsub transmission and distribution networks in urban areas. The R-APDRP (Restructured \nAccelerated Power Development & Reforms Programme) schem e was carried forward \nand subsumed under IPDS. State -wise infrastructure created under IPDS is at  \nAnnexure-II(a) and Annexure-II(b).  \n \nDPRs under IPDS were prepared circle -wise and accordingly projects were \nsanctioned for each Circle. The details of proje cts implemented under IPDS in State of \nKarnataka, circle-wise and district-wise, are attached as Annexure-III. \n \n(d) to (f) : All the States have reported 100% household electrification as on 31.03.2021. \nA total of 2.817 crore households have been electrifi ed since the launch of Saubhagya, \nupto 31.03.2021, including households of Scheduled Castes / Scheduled Tribes and \nother categories. State -wise & Year -wise details of household electrification since \nlaunch of Saubhagya Yojana are at Annexure-IV. \n \nThe SAUBHAGYA scheme was launched with the objective of connecting \nall willing households with electricity.  Projects under SAUBHAGYA are s anctioned \nacross all the States. \n \n********** \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN PARTS (a) TO (c) OF THE STATEMENT LAID IN \nREPLY TO STARRED QUESTION NO.  242 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA \nON 05.08.2021 REGARDING ELECTRICITY TO POOR HOUSEHOLDS UNDER \nSAUBHAGYA \n*********** \n \nState-wise electrification of households since launch of Saubhagya  \nSl. No. Name of the States \n \nNo. of Households \nelectrified from \n11.10.2017 to \n31.03.2019 \n \nNo. of Households \nelectrified from  \n01.04.2019 to \n31.03.2021 \n \nTotal HHs \nelectrified as \non 31.03.2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1,81,930   1,81,930 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 47,089   47,089 \n3 Assam 17,45,149 2,00,000 19,45,149 \n4 Bihar 32,59,041   32,59,041 \n5 Chhattisgarh 7,49,397 40,394 7,89,791 \n6 Gujarat 41,317   41,317 \n7 Haryana 54,681   54,681 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 12,891   12,891 \n9 Jammu & Kashmir 3,77,045   3,77,045 \n10 Jharkhand 15,30,708 2,00,000 17,30,708 \n11 Karnataka 3,56,974 26,824 3,83,798 \n12 Ladakh 10,456   10,456 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 19,84,264   19,84,264 \n14 Maharashtra 15,17,922   15,17,922 \n15 Manipur 1,02,748 5,367 1,08,115 \n16 Meghalaya 1,99,839   1,99,839 \n17 Mizoram 27,970   27,970 \n18 Nagaland 1,32,507   1,32,507 \n19 Odisha 24,52,444   24,52,444 \n20 Puducherry 912   912 \n21 Punjab 3,477   3,477 \n22 Rajasthan 18,62,736 2,12,786 20,75,522 \n23 Sikkim 14,900   14,900 \n24 Tamil Nadu 2,170   2,170 \n25 Telangana 5,15,084   5,15,084 \n26 Tripura 1,39,090   1,39,090 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 79,80,568 12,00,003 91,80,571 \n28 Uttarakhand 2,48,751   2,48,751 \n29 West Bengal 7,32,290   7,32,290 \nTotal  2,62,84,350  18,85,374   2,81,69,724  \n  \n  \n*********** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II (a) \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN PARTS (a) TO (c) OF THE STAT