    "ls|LS17_S6_U_38": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF EXPENDITURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 38 \nMonday, July 19, 2021/Ashadha 28, 1943 (Saka)  \nDirect Benefit Transfer \n \n38. DR. T.SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Controller General of Accounts maintains all the details of Direct Benefit \nTransfer; \n(b) if so, the details of reports maintained; \n(c) the list of all types of reports being stored by the Ministry on D irect Benefit Transfer; \nand \n(d) the scheme-wise errors, amounts and numbers reported during Direct Benefit Transfer \nto beneficiaries during financial year 2020-2021. \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d)  : Public Financial Management System (PFMS) O/o Controller General of \nAccounts (CGA) , Ministry of Finance maintains the data pertaining to the D irect Benefit \nTransfer (DBT) transactions in respect of Central S ector Schemes (CS) and  Centrally \nSponsored Schemes (CSS)  which are on -boarded on PFMS by the \nMinistries/Departments of Government of India . There are 6 different DBT reports for \nmonitoring the payment status by the user Ministries/Depa rtments. The reports available \non PFMS portal are;  (i) DBT01: Beneficiaries Registration Status (ii) DBT0 4: District wise \nDBT Transaction summa ry (iii) DBT05: Scheme wise D BT Transaction Summary (iv) \nDBT07: State wise DBT Transaction Summary (v) DBT0 9: Scheme wise Performance \nReport for DBT Transactions (vi) D BT16: PAO -DBT Payment St atus. The scheme -wise \nerrors, amounts and numbers reported during Direct Benefit Transfer to beneficiaries are \ndealt appropriately by concerned Ministries/Departments. \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_39": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.39 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nCLOSING OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYA IN KERALA \n \n39.  ADV. A.M. ARIFF:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has received any proposal for closing down the \nproject sector Kendriya Vidyalaya attached to NTPC thermal power plant in \nKayamkulam in Kerala and if so, the status thereof;  \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to consider converting the above said \nKendriya Vidyalaya to a civil sector KV taking into consideration the interests \nof the students and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)  whether the Government proposes for opening more civil sector Kendriya \nVidyalayas in Kerala and if so, the steps taken/to be taken in this regard? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b) Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has informed that the Kendriya \nVidyalaya (KV) NTPC, Kayamkulam was opened in the year 1999 -2000 in project \nsector under the sponsorship o r funding by  NTPC, Kayamkulam mainly to cater to \nthe educational needs of the wards of the NTPC employees.  NTPC authorities at \nKayamkulam have conveyed that due to f inancial constraints as well as less number \nof wards of NTPC employees, NTPC would be  unable to continue the financial \nsupport to KV, NTPC Kayamkulam from the academic year 2022 -2023. The NTPC \nauthorities at Kayamkulam have conveyed that NTPC is ready to e xtend the \ninfrastructural facilities to KVS till a permanent alternate arrangement is made.  \n \n(c)  Opening of KVs is a continuous process. The proposals received from various \nsponsoring authorities found fulfilling the mandatory pre-requisites have to compete \nwith other such proposals under the “Challenge Method”. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_4": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 4 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th JULY, 2021 \n \nModel Degree Colleges \n \n†4. SHRI DEVJI M.PATEL: \n      SHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR: \n      SHRI JASWANT SINGH BHABHOR: \n  \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) refer to the reply given to the unstarred question number 1629 dated 02.03.2020 \nand State whether central assistance  has  been  given  to  States  for  setting  up \nmodel degree col leges to improve the standard of higher education in aspirational \ndistricts; \n(b) if so, the State-wise details thereof; \n(c)  the  details  of  the  budget  allocated  for  basic infrastructure  of  these  degree  \ncollege  especially  from technology  point of view; \n(d) whether the construction work of Model Degree College in Sirohi district, \nRajasthan/Satara and Solapur district, Maharashtra and Dahod district of Gujarat could \nnot  be  commenced  in  the  absence  of  budgetary allocations due to the lack o f \nbudget and if so, the time by  which  these  Model  Degree  Colleges  are  likely  be \nready in the said places and whether any time frame has been fixed for it? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Under the erstwhile scheme  of setting up of Model Degree College (MDC) in \n374 Educationally   Backward   Districts   (EBDs),   University   Grants   Commission   \nhad   approved assistance to 64 Model Degree Colleges.  The  Centrally  Sponsored  \nScheme  of  Rashtriya  Uchchatar  Shi ksha  Abhiyan  (RUSA),  which  subsumed  the  \nabove  mentioned  erstwhile  scheme  has  further  approved  central  support  to  130  \nModel Degree Colleges in unserved and underserved areas such as EBDs, aspirational \ndistricts etc. The State-wise details are annexed. \n\n \n(c) The unit cost for one Model Degree College approved under the scheme of \nRUSA is Rs. 12.00 crore. This includes both Central Share and State Share as per the \nfunding pattern applicable to the State /UT under the scheme. The funds can be util ised \nfor setting up of MDCs which includes construction of required infrastructural facilities \nsuch as classrooms, hostels, laboratories etc and also includes purchasing of \nnecessary equipments such as computers, lab equipments, etc. \n \n(d) An amount of Rs. 5.40 crores has been released by the Ministry as 1 st and 2nd \ninstalments of Central Share for the Government College, Reodar, Sirohi, Rajasthan  \nunder the scheme of RUSA . As per information received from the State, c onstruction \nwork was started on 14.03.201 9 and the construction of Academic Block, Library, \nSeminar Room etc are complete and the college is functional.  \n \nNo MDC has been approved  under RUSA  for Satara and Solapur districts in \nMaharashtra.  \n \nUnder the erstwhile scheme  of UGC  for setting up of MDC s, t wo MDCs were \napproved by UGC for the district of Dahod. Out of the Central share of Rs. 2.67 crores \nGovernment Science College, Zalod, Dahod has been released Rs. 2.39 crores. As per \ninformation received from the State, the construction of the college building has been \ncompleted. Government Arts College, Dhanpur , Dahod  has been released Rs. 1.33 \ncrores as Central Share. As per information received from the State, the tender process \nfor the same is under process. \n \n****\n\n \nANNEXURE \nAnnexure referred to in p arts (a) & (b) of Lok S abha Unstarred Question No. 4 to \nbe answered on 19th July, 2021 regarding Model Degree Colleges  asked by Shri \nDevji M.Patel, Shri Ranjeetsingh Hindurao Naik Nimbalkar, Shri Jaswant Singh \nBhabhor, Hon’ble MP \nState-wise details of Model Degree Colleges  approved under erstwhile scheme \nof setting up of Model Degree College (MDC) in 374 Educationally   Backward   Districts   \n(EBDs) by University   Grants   Commission and under the  Centrally  Sponsored  \nScheme  of  Rashtriya  Uchchatar  Shiksha  Abhiyan  (RUSA) \nName of State/ UT \nUnits approved by UGC \nunder the erstwhile \nscheme of Model Degree \nColleges \nUnits approved under \nRUSA \nAndhra Pradesh - 8 \nArunachal Pradesh 6 2 \nAssam 12 5 \nBihar - 4 \nChhattisgarh - 11 \nGujarat 19 - \nHaryana - 1 \nHimachal Pradesh - 2 \nJammu & Kashmir 8 3 \nJharkhand - 10 \nKarnataka - 2 \nKerala - 1 \nMadhya Pradesh - 10 \nMaharashtra 7 2 \nMeghalaya - 2 \nNagaland - 2 \nOdisha - 14 \nPunjab 11 3 \nRajasthan - 5 \nSikkim - 2 \nTelangana - 7 \nTripura - 4 \nUttar Pradesh - 26 \nUttarakhand - 3 \nAndaman and Nicobar     \nIslands - 1 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli \n& Daman and Diu 1 - \nTOTAL 64 130 \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_40": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Revenue \n \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.40 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY19, 2021 \nASHADHA 28, 1943(SAKA) \n \nCOLLECTION OF TAXES \n40. SHRI  D. K. SURESH:  \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   the details of total collection of various taxes including corporate tax and GST during the \nlast three years and the current financial year, tax category-wise; \n \n(b)   the details of total non-tax revenue collected by the Union Government during this financial \nyear so far; \n \n(c)   whether it is true that the tax collection has reduced during the current financial year; and \n \n(d)   if so, the response of the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)   The category -wise details of total collection of various taxes including corporate tax and \nGST during the last three years and the current financial year are as under: \n \nDirect Tax \n(Rs. in crore) \nHead FY  \n2018-19 \nFY  \n2019-20 \nFY  \n2020-21* \nFY \n2021-22* \nupto 30.06.2021 \nCorporation Tax 6,63,571.62 5,56,875.55 4,57,050.27 1,20,991.64 \nPersonal Income \nTax including \nWealth Tax etc.  \n4,62,453.19 4,81,461.38 4,71,401.81 1,20,154.78 \nSecurities \nTransaction Tax \n11,527.50 12,374.23 16,926.99 5,373.40 \n *Provisional  \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nIndirect Tax  \n \n                      (Rs. in crore) \nHead \n \nNet Non- GST \nFY  \n2018-19 \nFY  \n2019-20 \nFY  \n2020-21*  \nFY \n 2021-22* \n    Upto30.06.2021 \nCustoms duty  1,17,813  1,09,283  1,34,825 41,831 \nUnion Excise  2,31,045  2,39,452  3,89,677  1,01,564 \nST(arrears) 6,904 6,029 1,644 167 \nNet GST \nCGST  4,57,534  4,94,071  4,56,348  1,17,446 \nIGST(residual) 28,945 9,125 7,209 25,754 \nComp. Cess 95,081 95,553 85,191 24,636 \n*Provisional \n \n \n(b)  As per CGA Monthly Accounts, the provisional Non-Tax Revenue collection in FY 2021-22 is \nas under:  \n \n                                                                                           (Rs. in crore) \nHead Actual upto May, 2021 \nInterest Receipts 2759.42 \nDividend and Profit 99628.33 \nNon-Tax Revenue of U.T.s 222.59 \nOther Non-Tax Revenue 13802.10 \nTotal  1,16,412.44 \n \n \n(c)  No Sir. \n \n(d) Does not arise, in view of (c) above. \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_41": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *41\nANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\nPOWER PRODUCTION FROM SRISAILAM RESERVOIR\n*41. SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government of Andhra Pradesh has requested the Union Government to instruct the\nGovernment of Telangana to stop the power production from Srisailam Reservoir which is being\nundertaken against the decision of the Krishna River Management Board (KRMB);\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether any action has been taken on the request of the Government of Andhra Pradesh; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED\nQUESTION NO. *41 TO BE ANSWERED ON 22 nd JULY, 2021 ON “POWER PRODUCTION\nFROM SRISAILAM RESERVOIR” RAISED BY SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY.\n(a) Yes, sir.\n(b) to (d) The Hon'ble Chief Minister of Andhra Pradesh requested the Union Government to prevail upon\nthe State of Telangana to stop at once the indiscriminate drawal of water solely for power generation.\nThe KRMB, vide letter dated 15.07.2021 requested the Telangana State GENCO authorities to\nstop further release of water immediately through Srisailam Dam, Left Power H ouse as it was agreed that\nquantum of water drawn for power generation purpose is incidental to irrigation and drinking water needs.\nSubsequently, KRMB again vide letter dated 16.07.2021 has requested the Special Chief Secretary\nto Government, Irrigation & CAD Department, Government of Telangana to take immediate necessary\naction to stop further release of water through Srisailam Dam, Left Power House.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_42": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 42  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nBudget for Education \n \n42. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the specific budgetary allocation made for Financial Year 2021-22 to implement the National \nEducation Policy; \n \n(b) the budget required to implement various policy proposals of National Education Policy in \n2021. \n \n(c) the reasons for reduction in budgetary allocation from FY 2020-21 to FY 2021-22; \n \n(d) whether any timeline has been pursued by the Government to achieve the goals of NEP and if \nso, the details thereof ; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) and (b): Budgetary allocation for various Schemes and Non-schemes of both the Departments \n(D/o of School Education & Literacy and D/o Higher Education) of Ministry of Education for \n2021-22 is Rs. 93,224.29 crores. Various schemes/projects/programmes run by Ministry of \nEducation proposals are being aligned with National Education Policy 2020 (NEP 2020). In the \nlight of NEP 2020, specific budgetary allocation have been made in Budget 2021-22 for new \ninitiatives such as Academic Bank of Credit, Indian Knowledge System, Bhartiya Bhasha \nUniversity and institute of Translation, Multidisciplinary Education and Research Improvement in \nTechnical Education – EAP (MERITE), etc for the Financial Year 2021-22. \n \n(c): Budget Estimate of Ministry of Education for 2020-21 was Rs. 99,311.52 crores which was \nrevised to Rs. 85,089.07 crores. Budget Estimate for 2021-22 is Rs. 93,224.29 crores, which is an \nincrease of 9.6% over RE 2020-21.  \n\n \n \n \n(d) and (e): A number of action points/activities for implementation in school education as well as \nhigher education are mentioned in the National Education Policy 2020. NEP 2020 provides \ndifferent timelines as well as principles and methodology for its implementation. As per NEP 2020, \nimplementation of the Policy requires multiple initiatives and actions, which will have to be taken \nby multiple bodies in a synchronized and systematic manner. In this direction, Ministry and other \nimplementing agencies under its purview have already started taking initiatives towards \nimplementation of NEP 2020. Further, Ministry of Education has communicated to all States/UT \nGovernments and GoI Ministries/Departments to take appropriate measures for implementation of \nNEP 2020. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_43": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Economic Affairs \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.43 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY 19th JULY, 2021 \nNATIONAL SAVINGS SCHEME FUND \n \nNo.43 \n \nSHRI P RAVINDHRANATH \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:- \n \n(a) the details of utilization of money pooled in the National Savings Scheme Fund during the last \nthree years and the current year;  \n(b) the details of State/UT-wise collection of money under the Small Savings Schemes, through \nthe network of post offices, during each of the last three years and the current year; and  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to sustain the investor’s interest in the small saving \nschemes during the last three years and the current year? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)   The details of utilization of money pooled in the National Small Savings Fund during the \nlast three years and the current years may be seen at ‘Annexure-I’. \n \n(b)   The details of State/UT-wise collection of money under the Small Savings Schemes, \nthrough the network of post offices, during each of the last three years and the current year \nmay be seen at ‘Annexure-II’. \n \n(c)  The rate of interest on Small Savings Schemes is fixed based on returns on Government \nSecurities and some additional spread.  They continue to draw investor’s interest \nbecause of safety and attractive interest because of safety and attractive interest rates.  \nSchemes are also advertised in print and electronic media to bring awareness in general \npublic on regular basis.  \n***** \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \nUtilization of money pooled in NSSF from FY 2017-18 to FY 2020-21 (up to 13.07.2021) \nSr. No. Investment in \n2020-21 \n(upto \n13.07.21) \n2019-20 \n(Pro.) 2018-19 2017-18 \na Government of India Securities 156934.19 262413.32 138533.00 108661.00 \nb State Government Securities  7086.70 18702.87 12193.06 9