    "ls|LS17_S6_U_89": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA \n  STARRED QUESTION No. 89 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nGirls Education during Pandemic \n \n*89.      MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has assessed the damaging impact on girls’ education during lockdown \ndue to the COVID-19 situation; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that for online studies, the girls’ acc ess to computers or smart \nphones at home is limited; and \n \n(c) if so, the measures taken/being taken by the Government to address this issue keeping in view \nthe economic crisis being faced by the people due to COVID-19 Pandemic? \n \nA N S W E R \n \n MINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 89 TO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 ASKED BY                \nMS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE, HON’BLE MP REGARDING GIRLS EDUCATION \nDURING PANDEMIC. \n \n \n(a): Education being in the concurrent list of the Constitution, majority of the schools are under the \ndomain of respective State and UT Governments. A sample survey was conducted in the month of \nJuly, 2020 for schools of Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS)  and Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti \n(NVS) by the Department. Subsequently, further surveys were conducted in early 2021 regarding \navailability of various forms of digital devices with children. NVS has reported 92.5% and KVS \nhas more than 98% of students having  full or partial access to some digital device. Subsequently, \nNCERT has prepared ‘Alternative Academic Calendar’ and ‘Students’ Learning Enhancement \nGuidelines’. The guidelines suggest models for the following three types of scenarios to ensure that \nno child is deprived of reach of education and are available at Education Ministry’s and NCERT’s \nwebsite:- \na) Learning Enhancement during COVID-19 for students without digital devices;   \nb) Learning Enhancement during COVID -19 for students with limited accessibility  \nto digital devices & \nc) Learning Enhancement during COVID-19 for students with digital devices. \n \nStates/UTs were again advised vide letter dated 01.02.2021 to conduct survey of children \nwith access and without access to digital devices and also the special efforts made for the girl child. \n \n(b) to (c): The complexity and diversity of digital penetration in India could make it possible that \nchildren in rural areas including girls have inadequate access to computer or smart phones. A \ncomprehensive initi ative called PM eVIDYA has been initiated as part of Atma Nirbhar Bharat \nAbhiyaan on 17th May, 2020, which unifies all efforts related to digital/online/on -air education to \nenable multi-mode access to education. The initiative includes:  \n• DIKSHA (one nation , one digital platform) is the nation’s digital infrastructure for \nproviding quality e -content for school education in states/UTs and QR coded Energized \nTextbooks for all grades are available on it. \n• One earmarked Swayam Prabha TV channel per class from 1 to 12 (one class, one channel). \n• Extensive use of Radio, Community radio and CBSE Podcast- Shiksha Vani. \n• Special e -content for visually and hearing impaired developed on Digitally Accessible \nInformation System (DAISY) and in sign language on NIOS website/ YouTube. \n \nSeveral measures have been taken by the State and UT Governments amid the economic \ncrisis faced by the people due to COVID pandemic. States/UTs have prepared educational content \nin the form of audios and videos and telecasted on local Radio and TV channels or YouTube \nChannels. Bridge course material, workbooks, text books etc along with digital devices have also \nbeen provided to help the students. Mobile applications with pre -loaded content have been \ndeveloped. Also alternate academic calendar has been made for home based learning. Regular home \nvisits have been made by the teachers/volunteers and Mohalla classes or Community classes etc. \nhave been conducted to support education.   \n \n Further, due to COVID pandemic, since schools were closed, all enrol led children  \nat elementary level were provided Food Security Allowance comprising of food grains and dry \nration of pulses etc. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_9": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.9 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nKendriya Vidyalayas and Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas.  \n \n†9.  DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:  \nSHRI. RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI:  \nSHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA:  \nSHRI MAHESH SAHOO:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas and Kendriya Vidyalayas opened in \nthe country from 2014 onwards till date, Constit uency-wise including \nNabarangpur Parliamentary constituency of Odisha ;  \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to set up new JNV in every district/ sub -\ndivision/ block headquarters of Odisha in the coming years and if so, the details \nof the timeline therefor ;  \n(c)  whether any memorandum has been submitted for a new KV in Talcher District \nand if so, the reaction of the Government thereto ;  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to upgrade the existing KV and JNV by \nproviding digital libraries and modernized laboratory equipments etc. and if so, \nthe details thereof; and  \n(e)  whether the JNV in Odisha  are running with teachers on contract basis to meet \nthe shortage of teachers and if so, the details thereof and if not, the details of \nteacher strength in these schools? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) 158 Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) and 61 Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas (JNVs) \nhave been opened in the country from 2014 onwards till date. KVs and JNVs are not \nopened constituency-wise. State/UT -wise details of KVs and JNVs opened from 2014 \nonwards till date including State of Odisha are annexed. \n \n(b) The Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme envisages opening of one JNV in each district \nof the country. All the 30 districts of State of Odisha have already been covered with \nNavodaya Vidyalaya Scheme and an additional JNV h as also been functional in \nScheduled Tribes concentrated Malkangiri district.  \n\n  \n(c) Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has informed that the proposal for opening \nof a new KV in Talcher was received from the State Government of Odisha. The proposal \nhas been referred back to the State Government for removal of discrepancies.  \n \n(d) e-Granthalaya, a digital platform developed by National Informatics Centre provides a \ncomplete ICT solution with integrated library management software, a digital library module \nand cloud hosting environment. e-Granthalaya software has been installed in the libraries of \nthe KVs and 603 Navodaya Vidyalayas in the country. e -Granthalaya software is being used \nfor issue and receipt of library books and maintenance of records of books i n digital mode. \nUpgradation and modernization of laboratories in the KVs and JNVs is a continuous process \nand the work is taken up in a phased manner as per requirement. \n \n(e) Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti has informed that 206 teachers are engaged on \nshort-term contract basis to fill up the vacant teaching posts immediately to avoid any \nacademic loss of students.  \n****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 9 RAISED BY DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN, SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI, \nSHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA & SHRI MAHESH SAHOO , HON’BLE MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED ON 19/07/2021 REGARDING KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS \nAND JAWAHAR NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n \nNUMBERS OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS AND JAWAHAR NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \nOPENED IN THE COUNTRY FROM 2014 ONWARDS TILL DATE \n \nSl. No Name of the State/UTs \nKVs  \nJNVs \n \n1 Andhra Pradesh  8 0 \n2 Andman & Nikobar Island 0 1 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  5 1 \n4 Assam 3 0 \n5 Bihar 4 0 \n6 Chhattisgarh 10 11 \n7 Delhi 3 0 \n8 Gujarat 1 11 \n9 Haryana 8 1 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 2 0 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 2 5 \n12 Jharkhand 9 2 \n13 Karnataka 13 3 \n14 Kerala 3 0 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 20 4 \n16 Maharashtra 7 1 \n17 Manipur 2 0 \n18 Meghalaya 0 4 \n19 Mizoram 0 1 \n20 Nagaland 1 0 \n21 Odisha 10 0 \n22 Punjab 2 2 \n23 Rajasthan 14 2 \n24 Tamilnadu 3 0 \n25 Telangana 7 0 \n26 Tripura 0 4 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 15 7 \n28 Uttrakhand 2 0 \n29 West Bengal 4 1 \nTOTAL 158 61 \n***** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_90": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 90 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nREDUCTION IN ESI CONTRIBUTION \n \n*90.  SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Employees’ State Insurance Corporation has reduced \nthe ESI contribution by employees and employers; \n(b)if so, the number of employers benefitted by the financial increase; \n(c)whether the healthcare benefits under the scheme have changed \ndue to the change in the contribution rate; and \n(d)if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                     ****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 90 TO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \nASKED BY SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI REGARDING “REDUCTION IN \nESI CONTRIBUTION” \n \n(a) to (d): The monthly contribution payable to Employees’ State \nInsurance Corporation (ESIC) has been reduced with effect from \n01.07.2019 from 6.5% to 4% on monthly wages.   The rate of \ncontribution for employers’ share has been reduced from 4.75% to \n3.25% and employ ees’ share from 1.75% to 0.75 of monthly wages \nwithout compromising any of the benefits which are available to the \nemployees and their family members under the ESI Scheme.  \n  \nAs on 31.03.2020, the number of employers covered under \nESI scheme are 12,36,565 and number of employees and their  family \nmembers are 13.24 crore .  All the covered employers and employees \nhave benefitted from the decrease in rate of contribution.  \n \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_91": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nESI HOSPITALS \n \n*91.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n  SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether in view of the sizeable increase in the number of ESI \nbeneficiaries subsequent to expansion of ESI Scheme in newer \ngeographical areas, efforts are constantly being made to strengthen \nthe ESI medical care infrastructure to provide better medical \nservices in the vicinity of beneficiaries in the country; \n(b)the details in this regard along with success achieved so far; \n(c)whether the Government has made any study to find out the radius \nat which ESIC’s Health Care infrastructure like Hospital  or \nDispensary or Insured/ Insurance Medical Practitioner is presently \navailable in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)whether ESI is also getting assistance from private hospitals and \nmedical practitioners in the country; \n(e)if so, the details  of empanelled hospitals and medical practitioners, \nState-wise; and  \n(f)the details of contracts/agreements signed by ESI with various such \nhospitals and medical practitioners? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (f): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**                                                       ******* \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRE D TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f ) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 91 TO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \nASKED BY SHRI PRATAPRAO J ADHAV AND SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR \nSAHU REGARDING “ESI HOSPITALS” \n \n(a) to (f )   Subsequent to increase in number of beneficiaries due to expansion of \nESI Scheme in newer geographical areas since 2015, efforts are constantly made by \nEmployees’ State Insurance Corporation ( ESIC) to strengthen medical care \ninfrastructure in the country for providing better medical services in the vicinity of \nbeneficiaries.  In this regard, some of the initiatives  are as under:-  \n \ni. 7 New ESI hospitals with a total of 1160 beds have been commissioned.  \nii. Approval has  been granted for setting up of 50 new ESI Hospitals across the \ncountry with 4990 beds. \niii. Bed strength of other existing ESI hospitals has been enhanced. \niv. Approval has been granted for 92 new ESI Dispensaries. \nv. 68 new Dispensary Cum Branch Offices (DCBOs) have been made operational \nacross the country.  \nvi. Ceiling for transfer of funds to State Government s has been enhanced from \nRs.2150/- to Rs.3000/ - per Insured Person (IP) per annum to improve and \nstrengthen medica l service delivery.  This includes Rs.200 per IP per annum \nunder Project Implementation Plan (PIP) for incurring expenditure on equipment, \nmedical manpower, building infrastructure and strengthening of other ancilliary \nfacilities. \n \nConsidering beneficiaries’ needs and on ground feasibility, ESIC from time to time, \nhas revised its norms for setting up of ESI hospital/dispensary in accordance with \nminimum number of Insured Persons population required within specified radius of a \nlocation.  \n       ESIC has  al lowed  beneficiaries to avail medical benefi ts directly from ESI \nempaneled hospitals in case there are no direct medical healthcare facilities available \nwithin a radius of 10 K.M. from the residence of the Insured Person . Further, Insurance \nMedical Practit ioner (IMP) are engaged at suitable locations where ESI dispensary is \nnot available. \nESIC has empaneled 2270 tie -up hospitals and 1264 Insurance Medical \nPractitioners (IMPs) across the country. The state -wise details of such hospitals and \nIMPs are Annexure. \nContract agreement with tie -up hospitals and IMPs are entered into by Regional \noffices of ESIC based on a standard framework  \n \nIn addition, ESIC has collaborated with Ayushman Bharat -Pradhan Mantri Jan \nArogya Yojana (PMJAY) for providing medical services  through their empanelled \nhospitals in 152 districts in the country. \n \n   .  \n \n\n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF L OK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91  TO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 ASKED \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV  AND SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU \nREGARDING “ESI HOSPITALS” \n  \nState-wise details on N o. of Empaneled Hospitals  & Insurance Medical \nPractitioners (IMPs) \nSl. \nNo. STATE \nNumber of Empaneled \nTie-up Hospitals(Private \nhospitals)  \nNumber of Insured \nMedical \nPractitioners(IMPs)  \n1. Andhra Pradesh 101 36 \n2. Assam 26 16 \n3. Bihar 120 13 \n4. Chandigarh  13 0 \n5. Chhattisgarh 55 0 \n6. Delhi 108 0 \n7. Goa 2 4 \n8. Gujarat 92 1 \n9. Haryana 964 2 \n10. Himachal Pradesh 15 0 \n11. Jammu & Kashmir 21 0 \n12. Jharkhand 71 12 \n13. Karnataka 170 0 \n14. Kerala 123 0 \n15. Madhya Pradesh 92 3 \n16. Maharashtra 254 817 \n17. Meghalaya 2 0 \n18. Mizoram 4 0 \n19. Manipur 1 0 \n20. Odisha 27 01 \n21. Puducherry 11 0 \n22. Punjab 73 0 \n23. Rajasthan 281 52 \n24. Sikkim 1 0 \n25. Tamil Nadu 164 0 \n26. Telangana 122 46 \n27. Tripura 1 0 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 129 0 \n29. Uttrakhand 18 0 \n30. West Bengal 109 261 \n Total  2270 1264 \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_92": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 92 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nJOB SURVEYS \n \n*92. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government  is conducting surveys to capture job \ncreation in various establishments in the country and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(b)whether the socio -economic conditions of migrants are being \nsurveyed by the Government; \n(c)if so, the time by which the result of the se surveys is likely to be \nreleased in public domain;  \n(d)whether job surveys across establishments, physically in both \nmanufacturing and services sectors are also being done; and  \n(e)if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n \n(a) to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                                  ******* \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRE D TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e ) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 92 DUE FOR REPLY ON 26.07.2021 \nREGARDING JOB SURVEYS. \n \nYes Sir. The Government is conducting surveys to capture change in \nemployment in various establishments in the country. Labour Bureau, an \nattached office of the Ministry of Labour &  Employment, has been \nentrusted with the task of conducting the  All India Quarterly \nEstablishment based Employment Survey (AQEES). It measures the \nchange in employment in respect of establishments in nine selected \nsectors namely Manufacturing; Construction ; Trade; Transportation; \nEducation; Health; Accommodation & Restaurants; IT/BPO; and Financial \nService Sector.  \n \nAQEES has two components: \n \ni) Quarterly Employment Survey (QES) in respect of establishments \nemploying ten or more workers.  \nii) Area Frame Establishment Survey (AFES) in respect of \nestablishments employing nine or less workers. \n \nAn Expert Group has been constituted under the Chairmanship \nof renowned Statistician Prof. S.P. Mukherjee to advice on technical \naspects of the surveys.  \n \nGovernment of India has also entrusted Labour Bureau to \nconduct All India Survey on Migrant Workers. The objective of the survey \nis to generate basic quantitative information on migrant workers and the \nimpact of COVID -19 pandemic on their work. The survey will also t hrow \nlight on the socio -economic conditions of migrant workers. Data will be \ncaptured on their education level, vocational/technical training, gender, \nsocial group, accommodation (housing), consumption expenditure, \nincome, financial inclusion, and change i n income after migration. The \nsurvey will be paperless with the Computer Assisted Personal \nInterviewing (CAPI) technology where the enumerators and supervisors \nvisit the household duly equipped with a hand held electronic device that \nensures faster & easie r data collection, collation and compilation.  \nGovernment has planned to complete All India Survey on Migrant Workers \nin one year, after which results will be released. \n \nReport of All India Survey on Quarterly Establishment based \nEmployment Survey (AQEES) will be released  within 2 months from \ncompletion of the field survey.  Thereafter, AQEES report will be released, \nevery quarter.  \n******* ",
